🤖 [Qwen Edge] To send a `PUT` request in GoLang, you would typically use the `http` package from the standard library. Here is an example of how to do it:

```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"net/http"
)

func main() {
	url := "http://example.com:8080/endpoint"

	req, _ := http.NewRequest("PUT", url, nil)
	client := &http.Client{}

	resp, err := client.Do(req)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Println(err)
		return
	}

	defer resp.Body.Close()
	body, _ := ioutil.ReadAll(resp.Body)

	fmt.Printf("%s\n%s\n", body, string(body))
}
```

This example sends a `PUT` request to the URL "http://example.com:8080/endpoint" and prints out the response body. Note that for this example, you need to have a server running at http://example.com:8080/endpoint and it should be configured to handle HTTP PUT requests.

Please remember to replace `http://example.com`, `8080`, and `/endpoint` with your actual URL and port and endpoint name if they are different.

