🤖 [Qwen Edge] To sort an array in JavaScript, you can use one of several built-in methods provided by the language:

1. `Array.prototype.sort()`: This method sorts the elements of an array in place and returns undefined.

```javascript
let arr = [3, 5, 2, 7];
arr.sort((a, b) => a - b);
console.log(arr); // Output: [2, 3, 5, 7]
```

2. `Array.prototype.slice()` combined with `.sort()`: This method is useful for sorting an array of arrays by the first element of each inner array.

```javascript
let arr = [[1, 'a'], [4, 'b'], [2, 'c']];
arr.sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
console.log(arr); // Output: [[2, "c"], [1, "a"], [4, "b"]]
```

3. `Array.prototype.map()` combined with `.sort()` and `.map()`: This method can be used to sort an array of objects by one or more properties.

```javascript
let arr = [
  { name: 'John', age: 25 },
  { name: 'Alice', age: 30 },
  { name: 'Bob', age: 21 }
];

arr.sort((a, b) => a.age - b.age);
console.log(arr); // Output: [{name: "Bob", age: 21}, {name: "John", age: 25}, {name: "Alice", age: 30}]
```

4. `Array.prototype.forEach()` combined with `.sort()` and `.forEach()`: This method can be used to perform a custom sorting function on an array.

```javascript
let arr = [1, -2, 3, -4];
arr.sort((a, b) => {
  if (a > b) return 1;
  if (a < b) return -1;
  return 0;
});

console.log(arr); // Output: [-4, -2, 1, 3]
```

5. `Array.prototype.concat()` combined with `.sort()`: This method is useful when you want to sort an array of arrays.

```javascript
let arr = [[1, 'a'], [4, 'b'], [2, 'c']];
arr.sort((a, b) => a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
console.log(arr); // Output: [[2, "c"], [1, "a"], [4, "b"]]
```

Each method has its use case and might be more suitable depending on your specific needs.

